This paper sets out the programme behind my doctoral project. Psychotherapy research has long held that the therapeutic relationship is central to outcome, but measuring that relationship has mostly meant questionnaires administered after the session has ended.

We argue for a different approach: treating the session itself as multimodal time series data, and using machine learning to model how the exchange between therapist and patient unfolds moment to moment. The paper covers what can be extracted from audio and video of a session, which methods suit interaction data specifically rather than individual behaviour, and where the methodological and ethical difficulties lie.

Written with colleagues from the Department of Psychology at Stockholm University.
